<%# [image: id, size] for the Google Doc — see DocTemplate::Tags::ImageTag.
    Google's HTML import ignores CSS widths, so the size travels in the alt
    text as "[size]": config/scripts/Code.gs (updateInlinedImages) scales the
    image to that percentage of the page or table cell, then strips the marker
    and leaves the sheet's alt text as the image's alt text.

    <= 50%: inline where the tag sits, left-aligned. Text cannot wrap around
    an inline Google Docs image; only positioned images wrap, and Apps Script
    can neither set alt text on one nor convert an inline image into one
    without losing it. So the Gdoc keeps the alt text, and the text continues
    beside the image's bottom line instead of wrapping.
    > 50%: a centered paragraph, then the caption and credit right-aligned in
    9pt and a blank line. Alignment uses the `align` attribute, which survives
    the import where CSS text-align on a <p> does not. %>
<% if @tmpl[:wrap] %><img class="o-ld-image__img" src="<%= @tmpl[:image_src] %>" alt="[<%= @tmpl[:size] %>] <%= @tmpl[:alt] %>"/><% else %>
<p class="o-ld-image" align="center"><img class="o-ld-image__img" src="<%= @tmpl[:image_src] %>" alt="[<%= @tmpl[:size] %>] <%= @tmpl[:alt] %>"/></p>
<% if @tmpl[:caption].present? %>
  <p class="o-ld-image__caption" align="right"><%= @tmpl[:caption] %></p>
<% end %>
<% if @tmpl[:credit].present? %>
  <p class="o-ld-image__credit" align="right"><%= @tmpl[:credit] %></p>
<% end %>
<p class="o-ld-image__spacer">&nbsp;</p>
<% end %>
